Chapter: ProbeCompositeEq
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Structural equality on NON-recursive composites: a plain record, and a sum
 that does not contain itself. The sibling of `probe-recursive-eq`, and the
 one that says whether finding 66 is about recursion at all.

 `probe-recursive-eq` found that the zig plug answers `==` on a SELF-RECURSIVE
 sum by identity where bare metal answers by contents, and it was written
 because Update 52 made that case newly reachable -- before it, the x86 back
 end refused a recursive structural compare outright.

 This file asks whether the recursion mattered. `emit-zig-binary`'s IrEq arm
 special-cases Text and otherwise emits raw zig `==`, and every record is a
 POINTER in that plug (finding 10, all of them, not just the ones spelled
 `mutable`) -- so by inspection the same identity answer should come back for
 ANY composite, recursive or not, and should have been coming back long before
 Update 52. Bare metal has compared non-recursive sums structurally all along:
 `emit-sum-structural-eq` predates this Update, and the recursive case was
 refused precisely BECAUSE the non-recursive path inlines a field compare and
 inlining has no correct bound.

 If that reading is right, Update 52 did not create this divergence. It only
 widened it, and the ladder never saw the older half because NO TIER COMPARES
 TWO COMPOSITE VALUES. Every `==` in every existing tier compares a scalar --
 a list length, a field, an integer. That is the gap this file closes.

 The controls are the same two that made the recursive probe diagnostic. A
 pointer comparison gets `different contents` and `a value compared to itself`
 RIGHT, so a file carrying only equal-contents rows could not tell identity
 from a broken structural compare.

 Values are built through functions, never written as literals side by side,
 because tier 9 paid for that lesson: a one-line wrapper let the inliner fold
 two constructions into a tautology that would pass on any arm.

 The sum's second row keeps the SAME constructor and changes the payload, so it
 tests the payload compare rather than a tag compare -- `Hi 5` against `Hi 7`,
 not `Hi` against `Lo`.

 Expected on bare metal: yes, no, yes for each of the two shapes.

Section: Types

  Point = record { x : Integer, y : Integer }

  Side =
    | Lo (Integer)
    | Hi (Integer)

Section: Instrument

  show-yn : Boolean -> Text
  show-yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Shapes

  mk-point : Integer -> Point
  mk-point (n) = Point { x = n, y = n + 1 }

  mk-side : Integer -> Side
  mk-side (n) = if n > 0 then Hi n else Lo n

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni "-- a plain record, no recursion anywhere --"
   let a = mk-point 1
   in let b = mk-point 1
   in let c = mk-point 9
   in act
     print-line-uni ("equal contents, two objects: " & show-yn (a == b))
     print-line-uni ("different contents         : " & show-yn (a == c))
     print-line-uni ("a value compared to itself : " & show-yn (a == a))
   end
   print-line-uni "-- a sum that does not contain itself --"
   let d = mk-side 5
   in let e = mk-side 5
   in let f = mk-side 7
   in act
     print-line-uni ("equal contents, two objects: " & show-yn (d == e))
     print-line-uni ("same ctor, other payload   : " & show-yn (d == f))
     print-line-uni ("a value compared to itself : " & show-yn (d == d))
   end
  end
